2. The implementation of obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), is technically carried out in an integrated manner by related SKPD.

CHAPTER III

VIOLENCE

Article 5

1. Forms of violence against women and children include:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect;
e. exploitation; and/or
f. other violence.
2. Physical violence as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a is caused by acts that result in pain, injury, wounds or disability to a person's body, miscarriage, unconsciousness and/or cause death.
3. Psychological violence as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b is caused by acts that result in fear, loss of self-confidence, loss of ability to act, feelings of helplessness and/or severe psychological suffering to a person.
4. Sexual violence as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c is caused by:
a. acts in the form of sexual harassment;
b. forced sexual intercourse;
c. forced sexual intercourse in an unnatural or unwanted manner; and/or
d. forced sexual intercourse with others for commercial purposes and/or certain purposes.
5. Neglect as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d is caused by:
a. acts that result in the failure to meet the needs of children properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social, committed by parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for their care;
b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or look after children as they should be, committed by parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for their care;
c. acts of neglecting a person within the household scope, whereas according to the law applicable to them or due to an agreement or contract they are obliged to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person; and/or
d. acts that result in economic dependence by restricting and/or prohibiting decent work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.
6. Exploitation as referred to in paragraph (1) letter e is caused by:
a. acts that exploit economically or sexually with the intention of benefiting oneself or others;
b. acts with or without the victim's consent which include but are not limited to prostitution, forced labor or services, slavery or similar practices, oppression, extortion, utilization of physical, sexual, reproductive organs, or unlawfully transferring or transplanting organs and/or body tissues or utilizing the labor or ability of a person by another party to obtain benefits both material and immaterial; and/or
c. all forms of utilization of sexual organs or other body organs of the victim to obtain benefits, including but not limited to all prostitution or indecency activities.
7. Other violence as referred to in paragraph (1) letter f is caused by:
a. threats of violence include: any unlawful act in the form of speech, writing, images, symbols, or body movements, whether with or without using means that cause fear or restrict a person's fundamental freedom; and
b. coercion, includes: a situation where a person/victim is ordered to do something in such a way that the person does something contrary to their own will.

Article 9

1. the obligations and responsibilities of the community as referred to in Article 7 letter b, are organized in the form of community participation.
2. Forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (1), include:
a. forming family partners at the sub-district/village level by the community;
b. forming women and child protection units within community organizations;
c. conducting independent socialization of women's and children's rights;
d. providing first aid to victims; and
e. reporting to the competent authority if violence against victims occurs in their environment.
3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, private sector, and mass media.
4. Forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (2), are carried out responsibly in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

Article 23

1. The Regional Government through P2TP2A shall be obliged to carry out economic empowerment for women and children victims of violence, exploitation and/or trafficking or neglect.
2. Forms of empowerment of victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) include:

a. providing the necessary needs for entrepreneurship training for victims of violence, in order to increase knowledge, attitudes, and business skills;

b. facilitating the implementation of various job training and skills training;

c. providing assistance in developing productive economic businesses;

d. exploring cooperation with small, medium and large enterprises, as well as financial institutions to develop businesses of victims of violence;

e. seeking the provision of capital for victims of violence; and

f. expanding access to information and promoting the products of victims of violence.

CHAPTER IV

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILD VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

Article 6

1. Every woman and child who becomes a victim of physical violence, exploitation or human trafficking has the right to receive protection and services.
2. Protection and services for victims as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. complaints or requests for protection from the person concerned;
b. confidential handling or placement of victims in safe locations and houses whose whereabouts are kept confidential to avoid intimidation and threats;
c. medical services/health care in the form of treatment and recovery of injuries or physical condition of victims by medical personnel and paramedics;
d. medicolegal services for legal evidence;
e. protection by law enforcement officials;
f. provision of information regarding legal rights and obligations to victims or guardians of victims and identification of incidents;
g. continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage;
h. psychological assistance to restore the traumatic condition of victims and restore victims' self-confidence;
i. legal assistance for legal processes and obtaining compensation or restitution rights;
j. guarantee of rights related to status as family members and community members (social reintegration); and
k. economic independence services in the form of skills training and providing economic access so that victims can be independent.
3. In the case where the victim is a child, in addition to obtaining the rights as referred to in paragraph (2), they also receive special rights including:
a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma;
e. the right to obtain freedom; and
f. the right to obtain compensation from the perpetrator.

CHAPTER XII

INVESTIGATION

Article 30

1. In addition to general investigating officials, investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children in this Regional Regulation may also be carried out by Civil Servant Investigators whose appointment is in accordance with laws and regulations.
2. Civil Servant Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1), are authorized to:
a. receive reports or complaints from a person regarding the existence of criminal acts of violence against women and children;
b. take first action at that time at the scene and conduct examination;
c. interrogate a suspect and examine the suspect's identification;
d. seize objects and/or documents;
e. take fingerprints and photograph a person;
f. summon persons to be heard and examined as suspects or witnesses;
g. bring in experts needed in connection with case examination;
h. cease investigation after receiving instructions from the General Investigator that there is insufficient evidence, or the incident does not constitute a criminal act and subsequently through the General Investigator notify this matter to the public prosecutor, the suspect or their family; and/or
i. take other actions necessary for the smooth investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children, which according to law can be accounted for.
3. Investigators as referred to in paragraph (2), notify the commencement of investigation and submit the results of their investigation to the public prosecutor through the Investigating Official of the Police of the Republic of Indonesia, in accordance with the provisions stipulated in the Criminal Procedure Code.

